Title: Diagnosis

Details:
- The failing test constructs a CSVParser with a header of three columns "A", "B", "C" and parses a single record line "a,b". This produces a CSVRecord whose:
  - header mapping contains three entries: A→0, B→1, C→2
  - values array contains only two elements: ["a", "b"] (length 2)

- The test then calls shortRec.toMap().

- Implementation details:
  - CSVRecord.toMap():
    - Line 211–213:
      - public Map<String, String> toMap() {
          return putIn(new HashMap<String, String>(values.length));
        }
    - It delegates to putIn.
  - CSVRecord.putIn(M):
    - Line 179–185:
      - <M extends Map<String, String>> M putIn(final M map) {
          for (final Entry<String, Integer> entry : mapping.entrySet()) {
              final int col = entry.getValue().intValue();
              map.put(entry.getKey(), values[col]);
          }
          return map;
        }
    - This iterates over all header mappings and directly indexes into values[col] without any bounds checking.

- What happens during the test:
  - For headers "A" and "B", col is 0 and 1, so values[0] = "a" and values[1] = "b" succeed.
  - When it reaches header "C", col is 2, but values.length is 2, so accessing values[2] throws an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.

- Contrast with CSVRecord.get(String):
  - Lines 94–111 show that get(name) catches ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException and throws a more informative IllegalArgumentException about the mismatch between header index and record length. putIn lacks such handling.

Root cause:
- CSVRecord.putIn iterates through the entire header mapping and accesses values[col] without verifying that col is within values.length, leading to ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException when the record has fewer fields than there are headers.

